          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is anemia, and does it always mean low iron?",
          "answer": "**Anemia** means a lower than normal amount of hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. The Iron-deficiency anemia overview (General clinical reference) describes it this way.\n\n**No, anemia does not always mean low iron.** Iron deficiency is the most common cause worldwide, but it is one cause among several. The same overview also points out that in adults, low iron is a finding rather than a diagnosis. Iron is mostly lost through bleeding, so a doctor will usually want to find out where it went. Our sources did not describe vitamin B12, folate, or chronic disease as causes in any detail. The ICD-10-CM Chapter 3 summary (US clinical modification of ICD-10) does indicate that anemia of chronic disease and the anemia that comes with kidney failure are recognized categories, but it does not explain their causes.\n\nIf you have been told you have anemia, or your blood test shows low hemoglobin, the usual next step is to ask your clinician what type it is and what might be causing it. This matters because the cause decides the approach.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have black, tarry stool, blood in your stool or vomit, fainting, chest pain, or trouble breathing. The GI bleeding guidance (Gastroenterology and emergency medicine society guidance) says black stool should be treated as bleeding until a clinician has checked it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
